  },
  "grammar": {
    "title": "Ask anything in Spanish",
    "intro": "Spanish opens a question with an upside-down <b>¿</b> and closes it with <b>?</b>. To ask for information, start with a <b>question word</b> — and note that every question word carries a written <b>accent</b>: qué, quién, dónde…",
    "sections": [
      {
        "tag": "palabras",
        "heading": "The question words",
        "body": "Learn these and you can ask almost anything. All of them have an accent:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "qué",
            "what"
          ],
          [
            "quién / quiénes",
            "who (sing. / plural)"
          ],
          [
            "dónde",
            "where"
          ],
          [
            "cuándo",
            "when"
          ],
          [
            "cómo",
            "how"
          ],
          [
            "por qué",
            "why"
          ],
          [
            "cuánto / cuánta",
            "how much"
          ],
          [
            "cuántos / cuántas",
            "how many"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "¿<b>Qué</b> quieres?",
            "en": "What do you want?",
            "note": "qué = what"
          },
          {
            "es": "¿<b>Quién</b> es?",
            "en": "Who is it?",
            "note": "quién = who"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "orden",
        "heading": "Word order",
        "body": "The usual pattern is <b>¿ + question word + verb + …?</b> The subject often comes after the verb or is left out entirely:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "¿Dónde vives?",
            "Where do you live?"
          ],
          [
            "¿Cómo estás?",
            "How are you?"
          ],
          [
            "¿Cuándo trabajas?",
            "When do you work?"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "¿<b>Dónde</b> está el baño?",
            "en": "Where is the bathroom?",
            "note": "dónde + estar"
          },
          {
            "es": "¿<b>Cuántos</b> años tienes?",
            "en": "How old are you? (lit. how many years)",
            "note": "cuántos agrees with años"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "porque",
        "heading": "¿Por qué? vs porque",
        "body": "Don’t mix these up: <b>¿por qué?</b> (two words, with accent) means <b>why?</b>. <b>porque</b> (one word, no accent) means <b>because</b> and is the answer:",
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "— ¿<b>Por qué</b> estudias español? — <b>Porque</b> me gusta.",
            "en": "— Why do you study Spanish? — Because I like it.",
            "note": "question vs answer"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "Open with ¿ and close with ? — always both.",
      "Every question word has a written accent: qué, quién, dónde, cuándo, cómo, por qué.",
      "Order: ¿question word + verb + …? → ¿Dónde vives?",
      "¿Por qué? (two words) = why; porque (one word) = because."
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      {
        "w": "¿Como estás?",
        "r": "¿Cómo estás?",
        "why": "As a question word, cómo needs an accent."
      },
      {
        "w": "¿Por que estudias? Porqué me gusta.",
        "r": "¿Por qué estudias? Porque me gusta.",
        "why": "Why = por qué (two words, accent); because = porque (one word, no accent)."
      }
    ]
  },
